//! fail
//! stderr: fail/ffi/004_libc_signature_conflict.ft:19:21: error: conflicting declarations
// The directive above pins the file the error stands in, which is the whole
// question here: an error annotation pins the line alone, so a report that
// moved to the library's file would leave this test with an unannotated
// diagnostic and no statement of what was wrong (D14.2).
//
// Every closure holds std.rt, which imports std.libc, so std.libc's `extern`
// declarations are in every closure and D9.8's identity rule binds a program's
// own declaration of one of those C symbols to them, `own` included (D9.10,
// D17.13). std.libc declares `extern fn void free(void* own p);`, so the
// declaration below is the same ELF symbol reached through two types, which is
// the bug the identity rule exists to catch. A program that wants another
// spelling imports std.libc and calls it rather than redeclaring it.
//
// The error stands on this declaration and not on the library's, because
// std.rt is loaded as a root of the closure and so precedes the program in the
// dependency order this diagnostic reports against (module-system.md 13).
extern fn void free(void* p); //! error: conflicting declarations of extern 'free'

fn i32 main() {
    void* p = null;
    free(p);
    return 0;
}
